In this lesson, we'll look at equations that have rational coefficients, meaning those equations come with fractions. Don't worry, there's a really clean way to handle them. The idea is simple: first, you clear the fractions, then you solve a regular whole-number equation. Here's the recommended method. Find the lowest common denominator of all the fractions sitting in your equation. That's the smallest number that every denominator divides into nicely. Then, multiply every single term on both sides by that common denominator. Because multiplication preserves equality, if two things are equal, multiplying both by the same number keeps them equal. After this step, all your fractions disappear, and you're left with a straightforward linear equation you can solve using inverse operations like you normally would. There is another method where you keep the fractions and use inverse operations, but honestly, it's easier to make little slip-ups that way, so clearing the denominators first is the way to go. Now let's see it in action.